ABSD per-port patch for sbase 0.1 test (userland/README.md, "Patches").

Failure: test.c answers -e -r -w -x with faccessat(AT_FDCWD, path, mode,
AT_EACCESS). Neither the kit nor the POSIX adapter defines faccessat, so
the link fails (userland/DEMAND.tsv: test faccessat MISSING). And -nt/-ot
compare st_mtime, which is 0 for every file on ABSD (C-INTERFACE.md 11),
so both would silently answer false.

Change: -e -r -w -x call access(path, mode) (the adapter's, over the file
server: a probe open for R_OK/W_OK on a file). When access fails with
ENOSYS (W_OK on a directory: refused, SPEC A11) or EINVAL (X_OK on a
file: no execute permission exists), test reports the check as not
supported and exits 2 instead of answering false. With ABSD_NO_MTIME
defined (the ABSD build defines it while C-INTERFACE.md 11 holds), -nt
and -ot are refused the same way; temporary until R3 brings file times
to C, when the define and this hunk go.

Preserved: every answer the platform can give is the one upstream gives.
ABSD's identity is fixed (one uid, real == effective, SPEC A8), so
access() and faccessat(..., AT_EACCESS) agree; on Linux (the reference
build, no ABSD_NO_MTIME) access never fails with ENOSYS or EINVAL for
these modes and the program behaves as upstream. Exit status 2 is test's
own error status (enprintf(2, ...)). No adapter or kit change.

--- a/test.c
+++ b/test.c
@@ -48,6 +48,13 @@
 static int
 mtimecmp(struct stat *buf1, struct stat *buf2)
 {
+#ifdef ABSD_NO_MTIME
+	/* ABSD (userland/patches/test-access-mtime.patch): no modification time
+	   reaches a program (st_mtime is 0 for every file, C-INTERFACE.md 11),
+	   so -nt and -ot cannot be answered; they are refused, status 2. */
+	(void)buf1; (void)buf2;
+	enprintf(2, "-nt/-ot: file modification times are not available on this system\n");
+#endif
 	if (buf1->st_mtime < buf2->st_mtime) return -1;
 	if (buf1->st_mtime > buf2->st_mtime) return +1;
 #ifdef st_mtime
@@ -72,10 +79,26 @@
 static int unary_n(char *s) { return  *s; }
 static int unary_z(char *s) { return !*s; }
 
-static int unary_e(char *s) { return !faccessat(AT_FDCWD, s, F_OK, AT_EACCESS); }
-static int unary_r(char *s) { return !faccessat(AT_FDCWD, s, R_OK, AT_EACCESS); }
-static int unary_w(char *s) { return !faccessat(AT_FDCWD, s, W_OK, AT_EACCESS); }
-static int unary_x(char *s) { return !faccessat(AT_FDCWD, s, X_OK, AT_EACCESS); }
+/* ABSD (userland/patches/test-access-mtime.patch): access() in place of
+   faccessat(AT_FDCWD, ..., AT_EACCESS), which ABSD does not provide; the
+   identity is fixed (one real and effective uid), so the answers are the
+   same. A check the platform refuses is reported, never answered false:
+   ENOSYS (write check on a directory) and EINVAL (execute check on a file:
+   no execute permission exists) end test with status 2. */
+#include <errno.h>
+static int
+absd_access(char *s, int mode, char *op)
+{
+	if (!access(s, mode))
+		return 1;
+	if (errno == ENOSYS || errno == EINVAL)
+		enprintf(2, "%s %s: check not supported on this system: %s\n", op, s, strerror(errno));
+	return 0;
+}
+static int unary_e(char *s) { return absd_access(s, F_OK, "-e"); }
+static int unary_r(char *s) { return absd_access(s, R_OK, "-r"); }
+static int unary_w(char *s) { return absd_access(s, W_OK, "-w"); }
+static int unary_x(char *s) { return absd_access(s, X_OK, "-x"); }
 
 static int unary_t(char *s) { int fd = enstrtonum(2, s, 0, INT_MAX); return isatty(fd); }
 
